  "holistic_F_H": -1,
  "section_notes": "The final S2 sets a 5% limit for new models, but already-marketed models get a 36-month transition with no numeric interim cap, which is the main concession to Northwind's Hearth 10 (6.2%). Per-model results go to purchasers and regulators but not the public, satisfying both companies' disclosure red lines. S3 gives new units 36 months and retrofits 6 years, which favours Northwind's low shut-off coverage, while S5 keeps the 8/100k mandatory recall trigger that exposes Halcyon's Ember 8 (9.9/100k)."
